    "q-141459": {
      "dom_id": "step-q141459",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(v)=0$ for some vector $v\\ne 0?$ \n\nI. $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}$\n\nII. $T$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and $v\\ne 0,$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection and the matrix $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
